            }
            
          </style><script type="application/ld+json" id="page-json-ld-0" data-next-head="">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"NewsArticle","mainEntityOfPage":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/recipes/article/orzo-salad-zucchini-corn-22322315.php","url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/recipes/article/orzo-salad-zucchini-corn-22322315.php","headline":"Recipe: Zucchini, Corn and Peach Orzo Salad","articleSection":"Food/Recipes","publisher":{"@type":"NewsMediaOrganization","name":"San Francisco Chronicle","logo":{"@type":"ImageObject","width":600,"height":60,"url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/sites/premiumsfgate/ldjson_logo.png"},"location":{"@type":"Place","name":"San Francisco Chronicle","address":{"@type":"PostalAddress","addressLocality":"San Francisco","addressRegion":"CA"}},"url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com","sameAs":["https://www.facebook.com/SFChronicle/","https://twitter.com/sfchronicle","https://www.instagram.com/sfchronicle/","https://www.linkedin.com/company/sfchronicle"]},"description":"Basil vinaigrette ties together peaches, corn, Parmesan-baked zucchini and halloumi in a produce-packed orzo salad.","image":{"@type":"imageObject","width":2048,"height":1365,"url":"https://s.hdnux.com/photos/01/66/55/13/31118019/5/rawImage.jpg","thumbnailUrl":"https://s.hdnux.com/photos/01/66/55/13/31118019/5/square_small.jpg","caption":"Flavorful, easy to transport and tasty the day after, this abundant summer salad is perfect for summer gatherings."},"articleBody":"Shades of green, red, orange, yellow, purple, magenta, pink greet you at the farmers market this time of year. Each stall brims with a bountiful rainbow of produce, offering an endless array of colors and textures and flavors — crisp, tender, juicy, sweet and earthy. This is California summer at its peak: colorful, abundant and endlessly inspiring. Inspired by all that color and texture, this zucchini, corn and peach orzo salad is a keeper, one that will be on repeat by anyone who makes it.  Produce-rich meals have always been a part of my dinner table, and that appreciation for fruits and vegetables has naturally been passed down to my children. Growing up surrounded by a variety of seasonal produce has made them open-minded eaters, eager to try new ingredients and flavors. Their enthusiasm inspires me in the kitchen, encouraging me to create dishes that celebrate fresh produce in delicious ways. There is something about the combination of zucchini, sweet corn and juicy peaches that just works miraculously well. Another dish that my family demands every summer, for instance, is my cheese toast featuring them, along with some spicy green chiles and cilantro, on sourdough. My orzo salad starts off with the most delicious basil vinaigrette. Made in a high speed blender, it can be stored in the fridge for up to 2 weeks — if it lasts that long. Next comes the Parmesan-baked zucchini, where zucchini is tossed in olive oil, salt and pepper, and baked in the oven after a generous sprinkle of Parmesan on each side. Flip them halfway through, another sprinkle of Parmesan and bake them to golden perfection.  Orzo is an Italian pasta that looks like a long grain of rice or barley and is made with durum wheat semolina or flour. Cook it per the package instructions and cook the corn over a grill or a gas stovetop. If you do not have either, you can strip the corn off the cob, sauté it in a pan with a little bit of butter for a few minutes until golden brown.  The peach is cut simply, into small cubes. Feel free to substitute a white peach or nectarine as well. I love the pop of color from the crimson peel. The salad also has halloumi, a semi-hard, briny cheese from Cyprus that has a high melting point, allowing it to be grilled or pan seared, which gives the outside a chewy texture with soft interiors.  All of the ingredients, along with finely chopped oregano and coarsely chopped pistachios, are tossed together with the basil vinaigrette. The salad is garnished with basil leaves and some more pistachios for good measure.  The best part about this orzo salad is how easy it is to transport for a summer get-together. It is best enjoyed with a light wine such as a Riesling. Zucchini, Corn and Peach Orzo Salad Serves 6 to 8 With summer produce at its peak, make this absolutely delightful and fresh salad, with oven-baked Parmesan zucchini, grilled corn, sweet and juicy peaches and grilled halloumi. An herbaceous basil vinaigrette ties it all together. This salad will be perfect for your summer soiree.  Basil vinaigrette 1 cup fresh basil, tightly packed 3 tablespoons lemon juice 2 tablespoons sherry or champagne vinegar 2 garlic cloves 1 teaspoon black pepper ¾ teaspoon salt 1 tablespoon honey ½ cup extra-virgin olive oil Parmesan-baked zucchini 1 large or 2 medium-sized zucchini, sliced vertically into half, then sliced into ¼-inch slices 2 tablespoons olive oil ½ teaspoon salt ½ teaspoon black pepper ½ teaspoon garlic powder ⅓ cup grated Parmesan  Orzo salad 8 ounces halloumi, cut into ⅓-inch slices 2 cups orzo, cooked per packaged instructions 1 corn on the cob, grilled 2 yellow peaches, cut into small cubes 2 tablespoons oregano, finely chopped ⅓ cup pistachios, coarsely chopped Garnish Basil leaves Yellow peach slices Make the basil vinaigrette: Add all the ingredients in a high-speed blender and blend to a smooth vinaigrette. Pour it into a glass jar and refrigerate until ready to use. Make the Parmesan-baked zucchini: Preheat the oven to 400 degrees. Spray a half baking sheet with cooking spray. Add the sliced zucchini, olive oil, salt, pepper and garlic powder and toss until well combined. Lay them flat such that they are not overlapping. Sprinkle half of the Parmesan onto each zucchini slice. Bake for about 20 to 25 minutes or until the zucchini is golden brown in color.  Using a small offset spatula, flip them over and sprinkle the Parmesan on the slices. Bake for another 20 to 25 minutes or until the zucchini is golden brown in color. Cool completely as it will turn crisp. Grill the halloumi: Spray cooking spray on a grill pan on medium heat. Add the halloumi slices, not overlapping each other, and grill for about 2-3 minutes, or until you see golden brown grill marks. Flip and repeat on the other side to get the grill marks. Set aside until ready to toss in the salad. Combine the ingredients: In a large platter, add the freshly cooked orzo, while still warm; grilled corn; peaches; grilled halloumi; and oregano. Mix in most of the pistachios, setting about 2 tablespoons aside. Pour the basil vinaigrette all over the orzo salad and toss until well combined. Garnish with the chopped pistachios, basil leaves and yellow peach slices. Serve at room temperature. Note: This dish can be made a day ahead and served at room temperature the next day.","datePublished":"2026-07-03T11:00:00Z","dateModified":"2026-07-03T11:00:19Z","author":[{"@type":"Person","name":"Amisha Gurbani"}],"isAccessibleForFree":false,"hasPart":{"@type":"WebPageElement","isAccessibleForFree":false,"cssSelector":".articleBody"},"inLanguage":"en"}</script><script type="application/ld+json" id="page-json-ld-1" data-next-head="">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"WebPage","breadcrumb":{"@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":"1","item":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.sfchronicle.com","url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com","name":"Home"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":"2","item":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/","url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/","name":"Food"}},{"@type":"ListItem","position":"3","item":{"@type":"WebPage","@id":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/recipes/","url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/recipes/","name":"Recipes"}}]},"publisher":{"@type":"NewsMediaOrganization","name":"San Francisco Chronicle","url":"https://www.sfchronicle.com/","address":{"@type":"PostalAddress","addressLocality":"San Francisco","addressRegion":"CA"},"logo":{"@ty